            <ArticleTitle>Risk factor medicalization, hubris, and the obesity disease.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>143-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1353/nib.2014.0043</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The essays on obesity in this issue frequently refer to the recent American Medical Association (AMA) declaration of obesity as a disease. In response to these essays, I describe and explore the significance of 'risk-factor medicalization' and how negative unintended consequences with this approach to disease modeling are exemplified in many of the essays. I also relate the essays' content to the issue of physician hubris in the face of their own helplessness in aiding the obese patient.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
